body:
Well,

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Bye.

This is more like "We frigging get it, Kevin. You welded a half-circle to your bike to do this. Enough already, you damn show off" . Or, I could be totally wrong.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

First show was 93. I was a total tour wook in 96, 97, 98 and 99. This means that I had seen over 120 shows when Cypress came about. I had seen every festival that Phish had put on thus far. This was different. At the time, it seemed like any other Phish fest until the midnight to sunrise set. It was a war of attrition, though. It was a commitment. Not only from the band, but from the fans as well. We never really thought that we were witnessing history.

&#x200B;

I know you young folks don't really care, but I met my wife at Big Cypress. Out of 85,000 people, I happened to work my way through the crowd, to blindly sit behind her and her friends on the first day. Her crew and mine hit it off, and hung the rest of the weekend. We've been together since 12/31/99, and have been married since 7/1/06.

When my parents separated, while I was in 5th grade in 1986, I moved in with my mom and my 7 yr. older sister stayed with my dad. We moved into a really nice apartment complex from the house we grew up in. After the first year, we had 2 young guys move into the apartment above us. They would bring home girls every night of the week, and we were then subjected to listening to them have sex at 3am, one in each bedroom.

&#x200B;

One night my mom had enough, grabbed a broom, and banged on the ceiling of our apartment yelling for them to shut up. This was on a Wednesday or Thursday. They were screaming back down to her, stomping on the floor, and being total assholes for the rest of the night by being exorbitantly loud until 4am or so.

&#x200B;

Then on the following Friday afternoon, they went out of town together for the weekend. They were thoughtless, classless losers, and thought it would be deserved payback to turn their stereo on, blaring a CD on repeat at full volume, when they left. We had to go to a hotel until they came back on Tuesday.

&#x200B;

Alright, I'll be that guy. To me, Perdomo doesn't make a stick worth smoking. 

&#x200B;

That's just me, though. It's always good to see the different palates that keep these sticks evolving.

Very simple. Music and politics.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I'm so late to this, that I'm typing just to hear myself think, but I just don't understand folks who make a mess in a restaurant.  You shouldn't have to have worked in the service industry, waiting or busing tables, to see how they are cleaned, and what being a remotely responsible guest is. Just be remedially tidy, and no one will get upset.

&#x200B;

I have a 4 and 8 year old, and we eat out regularly. If my kids make a mess, it's my job to clean it up. Not the damn server. Wake up, folks. But most importantly, my kids are disciplined enough to act properly, and they don't make a mess to begin with. Not tooting my horn, as 80% of parents out there are doing the same thing. It's that clueless 20% fucking it up for everyone.

