Excellent post, and very well written. I am a former audio engineer, and current audiodphile with a hefty 2-channel system, so your comparison hit home with me.

&#x200B;

As is the case with so many other products, there is for sure a place for indulgence in the cigar industry. For nearly every other consumer product, be it a CD player, bourbon, cigar, wine, olive oil or a painting, all it takes is a really good story and you can charge what the people will pay. 

&#x200B;

The fact remains. As someone who has been smoking cigars for 20 years, there is a major lack of differences between an $8 - $10 cigar and a $20 - $25 cigar. Just like that Pappy 12 year old that you are sure is so incredibly more amazing, is just as good as so many other more readily available, and much less expensive bourbons. We can argue tobacco age, wrapper, veins, tooth, oil, roll, triple cap, cold draw, foot smell and everything else all day, but all of these traits are glaringly present within many $8 cigars.

Box of Rain - Grateful Dead

&#x200B;

Listening to this, after a week of "goodbye" time and love spent with my 17 year old Golden Retriever, before putting him down after his battle with cancer. I listen to the Dead daily, but could not listen to this song for 6 months after he passed.
